1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,613
A new file. Who can read it? Who can change it? You never chose, yet Linux decided.

2
00:00:05,633 --> 00:00:08,892
The decision comes from one small number: the umask.

3
00:00:09,362 --> 00:00:12,222
In this video: manage default file permissions.

4
00:00:16,742 --> 00:00:19,325
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

5
00:00:19,795 --> 00:00:21,652
Manage default file permissions.

6
00:00:22,122 --> 00:00:25,590
We'll read the current umask, work out the permissions it gives,

7
00:00:25,610 --> 00:00:29,078
change it for one user, and see where the system default is set.

8
00:00:36,598 --> 00:00:42,356
Every new file starts from a maximum: 666 for a file, 777 for a directory.

9
00:00:42,376 --> 00:00:44,777
Files never start with execute.

10
00:00:44,797 --> 00:00:50,242
Then the umask takes permissions away, digit by digit. Take umask 027.

11
00:00:50,262 --> 00:00:54,771
The owner loses nothing. The group loses 2, the write bit.

12
00:00:54,791 --> 00:01:00,471
Others lose 7, everything. So a file ends as 640, and a directory as 750.

13
00:01:04,941 --> 00:01:08,424
The umask only removes permissions. It never adds any.

14
00:01:08,444 --> 00:01:12,900
And it only affects files created after the change, not the old ones.

15
00:01:22,420 --> 00:01:27,676
First, the current umask. The command umask alone prints it:

16
00:01:27,696 --> 00:01:34,886
0022. Add -S, for symbolic, to see it as letters: user rwx, group r-x, others r-x.

17
00:01:34,906 --> 00:01:38,667
Those are the permissions that are allowed.

18
00:01:38,687 --> 00:01:43,943
Now we create a file with touch, and a directory with mkdir.

19
00:01:43,963 --> 00:01:50,362
ls -l -d lists them without opening the directory. The file is rw-r--r--:

20
00:01:50,382 --> 00:01:57,309
644. The directory is rwxr-xr-x: 755. The dot at the end is the SELinux marker.

21
00:01:57,329 --> 00:02:01,178
We come back to SELinux later in the course.

22
00:02:05,698 --> 00:02:06,931
Now we change it.

23
00:02:06,951 --> 00:02:12,386
Typing umask 027 changes only the current shell, and it is lost at logout.

24
00:02:12,406 --> 00:02:16,219
In this lab every command runs in its own new shell,

25
00:02:16,239 --> 00:02:19,315
so we make it permanent the way you would:

26
00:02:19,335 --> 00:02:23,369
we add the line to the file .bashrc in the home folder.

27
00:02:23,389 --> 00:02:29,340
The echo command prints the text, and the two arrows, greater-than, greater-than,

28
00:02:29,360 --> 00:02:34,057
append it to the end of the file. tail -n 1 shows the last line.

29
00:02:34,077 --> 00:02:39,733
Now umask prints 0027, and the symbolic form shows group r-x, others nothing.

30
00:02:39,753 --> 00:02:42,387
This changes the lab user's .bashrc.

31
00:02:46,907 --> 00:02:55,286
Now new files. touch creates file2, and mkdir creates dir2. The file is rw-r-----:

32
00:02:55,306 --> 00:03:01,124
640. The directory is rwxr-x---: 750. Others get nothing.

33
00:03:01,144 --> 00:03:03,890
Exactly what we calculated.

34
00:03:08,410 --> 00:03:11,457
Where does the system default come from?

35
00:03:11,477 --> 00:03:16,978
In the file /etc/bashrc, one line sets the umask to 022 when it is zero.

36
00:03:16,998 --> 00:03:20,735
And in /etc/login.defs, the setting UMASK is 022.

37
00:03:20,755 --> 00:03:25,642
A change for one user belongs in their own .bashrc, like we did.

38
00:03:25,662 --> 00:03:29,553
System-wide changes affect everyone, so be careful.

39
00:03:34,073 --> 00:03:37,509
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

40
00:03:39,029 --> 00:03:45,522
We reboot. The umask is still 0027, because the line is in .bashrc.

41
00:03:45,542 --> 00:03:48,438
A new file is again rw-r-----.

42
00:03:55,958 --> 00:04:00,206
Your turn. The lab silently creates the user ivan first.

43
00:04:00,226 --> 00:04:05,616
Make the umask 077 permanent for ivan, with a line in his .bashrc file.

44
00:04:05,636 --> 00:04:09,122
Run the checker, then reboot and run it again.

45
00:04:19,592 --> 00:04:21,343
Try before you open the solution.

46
00:04:26,863 --> 00:04:30,726
Next up: configure key-based authentication for SSH.

47
00:04:31,196 --> 00:04:34,098
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
